---     the *door* made about it is the other. The decision cannot be recomputed later — the sampler
---     that answered it may be a different setting by then, and a decision re-made from a new
---     setting is a decision that disagrees with itself — so it is stored rather than derived.
---     There is a second decision, at the exit, but it is made from the outcome and from this one
---     (`Context::exported`), not from a fresh roll.
---   * **the shape of "no trace".** A job submitted before this migration and a job submitted by a
---     caller who asked for nothing are both untraced, and a column that cannot say so will say
---     `false` about one of them: nullable for the id, defaulted for the decision, and the two
---     together mean "nobody asked". `jobq/3.23-online-schema` is the step with an answer for doing
---     this to a table that is already big.
---   * **the id's type.** Thirty-two lower-case hex characters is an external contract (the format's
---     grammar, which `src/engine/trace.rs` enforces on the way in), and the column is where an
---     operator and a query meet it. Text stores what the wire carries; a `uuid` column would store
---     something the person reading the header cannot see.
+-- `trace_id` is nullable and `trace_sampled` defaults to false, which is the honest shape for both
+-- kinds of job that have no trace: the ones submitted before this migration and the ones a caller
+-- submitted without asking to be traced. A row with no id is a row nobody asked about; a row with an
+-- id and `false` is a trace that exists and is not kept, which is a different sentence and worth
+-- being able to write down.
 --
--- The index is part of the answer rather than an afterthought: "everything that happened in this
--- trace" is the query an operator runs at three in the morning, and it should be a lookup. Consider
--- what to do about the rows that have no trace, because most of them will.
+-- The id is text rather than `uuid` for the same reason the trail stores its kinds as text: the
+-- format's grammar is an external contract — thirty-two lower-case hex characters, never all
+-- zeroes — and the column is where a person and a query meet it. `src/engine/trace.rs` refuses an
+-- id that is not that, and a row that got in another way is refused when it is read back rather
+-- than handed on as a trace nobody can follow.
+
+alter table jobs add column trace_id text;
+alter table jobs add column trace_sampled boolean not null default false;
+
+-- The partial index is what makes "everything that happened in this trace" a lookup rather than a
+-- scan, and it is partial because the rows that matter are the ones that have an id: every job
+-- submitted before this migration, and every job nobody traced, would otherwise be an entry
+-- pointing at nothing.
+create index jobs_trace on jobs (trace_id) where trace_id is not null;
